      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: configure model providers from the Control UI settings page",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nModel providers could not be configured from the Control UI. The Settings → Model Providers page was a read-only dashboard: no way to store an API key, no way to see whether a credential comes from config or an environment variable, no way to test whether a provider actually works, and no way to change default models without editing `openclaw.json` or rerunning CLI onboarding. This is the Dashboard half of #81961.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nManaging several providers (primary + fallback + cheap/utility models across cloud and local providers) is a normal setup, and the gateway already had all the underlying machinery: `config.patch` merge-patches for `models.providers.<id>.apiKey`, the CLI live-probe engine (`openclaw models status --probe`), and `models.authLogout`. This PR surfaces that machinery in the settings page instead of inventing new persistence:\n\n- **New `models.probe` gateway RPC** (operator.admin, additive protocol change, no version bump) reusing the CLI auth-probe engine. Direct config/env keys are probed through a synthetic profile in an isolated temp agent dir that is disposed afterwards, so plaintext never touches the real auth-profile store and errors are redacted + mapped to fixed status buckets.\n- **`models.authStatus`** now reports API-key provenance per provider (`apiKey: { source: \"config\" | \"env\", envVar? }`, never the value) and per-profile `logoutSupported`; `models.authLogout` accepts optional `profileIds` for targeted logout that preserves API keys and unrelated profiles.\n- **`models.list`** gains an opt-in `includeProviderCapabilities` flag exposing `apiKeySupported` per entry (from provider plugin auth contracts) so the UI only offers key entry where a key can work.\n- **UI**: provider cards get credential-source summary, Set/Replace/Remove API key (masked input, RFC 7396 merge-patch with `null` delete, secret never rendered back), Test connection with per-credential status + latency, and confirm-gated logout. New Add-provider flow for known-but-unconfigured catalog providers. New Default models card managing `agents.defaults.model` (primary + ordered fallbacks) and `agents.defaults.utilityModel` (Automatic vs explicit vs Disabled — empty string is the existing disable contract). Everything gated on operator.admin and degraded gracefully on older gateways (method-not-found hides probing).\n\nNo new config keys or env vars; the only persisted surfaces are the existing `models.providers.<id>.apiKey`, `agents.defaults.model`, and `agents.defaults.utilityModel`.\n\n## User Impact\n\nFrom Settings → Model Providers you can now: store/replace/remove provider API keys, see exactly which credential each provider uses (OAuth/token profiles, config key, or env var by name), live-test a provider connection and get a categorized result (auth / rate-limit / billing / timeout / format) with latency, log out OAuth/token profiles without nuking API keys, add a new provider by picking it from the catalog and pasting a key, and set the default/fallback/utility models. Docs: `docs/concepts/model-providers.md` gains a Control UI section.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Focused suites (Blacksmith Testbox): gateway protocol, auth profiles, probe engine + targets, `models.list`, `models.authStatus`, new `models.probe`, model-providers data/mutations/view — 351 tests passed; Control UI Playwright E2E (`ui/src/e2e/model-providers.e2e.test.ts`) 2 passed; `tsgo` core/ui/test lanes green; `pnpm protocol:check` green after regenerating Swift/Kotlin clients.\n- i18n: all new `en.ts` keys synced across all 20 locales with `fallbacks=0`.\n- Exact run IDs and re-run proof on the current head are recorded in the PR checks / comments before merge.\n\nCloses #81961\n",
